+const faqData = [
+  {
+    question: "Why do cats purr?",
+    answer: `Cats purr for a variety of reasons beyond contentment. While a relaxed cat on your lap is the classic image, cats also purr when they're anxious, injured, or giving birth. The vibrations (typically 25–150 Hz) may promote bone healing and muscle repair — making the purr a built-in self-soothing mechanism. It's essentially a cat's Swiss Army knife of communication.`,
+  },
+  {
+    question: "Do cats really always land on their feet?",
+    answer: `Mostly yes, thanks to a remarkable ability called the <strong>righting reflex</strong>. Cats have a flexible spine and no functional collarbone, which lets them rotate their bodies mid-air to orient feet-downward in as little as 30 cm of free fall. That said, they can still be injured — especially from very short or very high falls — so "always" is an overstatement.`,
+  },
+  {
+    question: "Why does my cat bring me dead animals?",
+    answer: `This is a gift — genuinely. Cats are instinctive hunters, and bringing prey to you is a behavior inherited from wild ancestors who shared food with their social group. Some behaviorists also suggest cats are "teaching" you to hunt, since you seem incapable of catching your own mice. The polite response is to accept graciously (then dispose discreetly).`,
+  },
+  {
+    question: "What does slow blinking mean?",
+    answer: `A slow blink from a cat — sometimes called a <strong>"cat kiss"</strong> — signals trust and relaxation. In feline body language, closing your eyes near another creature is a profound act of vulnerability. When your cat slow-blinks at you, they're indicating they feel safe. You can slow-blink back; studies suggest cats respond positively and approach humans who return the gesture.`,
+  },
+  {
+    question: "Why are cats so obsessed with boxes?",
+    answer: `Boxes satisfy several deep feline needs at once:
+    <ul>
+      <li><strong>Security</strong> — enclosed spaces limit angles of attack, reducing stress.</li>
+      <li><strong>Thermoregulation</strong> — cardboard insulates; cats prefer ambient temps of 30–38 °C.</li>
+      <li><strong>Ambush instinct</strong> — a box is the perfect hide from which to pounce.</li>
+    </ul>
+    Research from shelters shows cats given boxes adapt to new environments significantly faster.`,
+    html: true,
+  },
+  {
+    question: "How do cats communicate with humans vs. other cats?",
+    answer: `Adult cats rarely meow at each other — that vocalization evolved almost exclusively to communicate with <em>humans</em>. With other cats, they rely on body language, scent marking, chirps, trills, and hissing. With us, they've learned that meowing gets results (food, attention, door-opening). Each cat can also develop a unique "vocabulary" tailored to their specific human's responses.`,
+  },
+  {
+    question: "Why do cats knock things off tables?",
+    answer: `Several theories exist, none mutually exclusive:
+    <ul>
+      <li><strong>Curiosity</strong> — batting objects tests whether they're prey worth chasing.</li>
+      <li><strong>Attention-seeking</strong> — your dramatic reaction is rewarding.</li>
+      <li><strong>Boredom</strong> — an under-stimulated cat will create its own entertainment.</li>
+    </ul>
+    The best prevention is enrichment: puzzle feeders, window perches, and regular interactive play.`,
+    html: true,
+  },
+  {
+    question: "Are cats truly solitary animals?",
+    answer: `It's complicated. Domestic cats descended from largely solitary wildcats, but centuries of living around humans and food sources have made them more socially flexible than their ancestors. Many cats enjoy companionship — feral colonies cooperate in raising kittens. Individual personality varies enormously; some cats thrive with feline friends, others strongly prefer being the only pet.`,
